    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Kant's political theory is not solely an ideal theory of right [Recht] that deduces political goals a priori, but simultaneously a "nonideal theory" concerned with the contingent variables of politics -- politically salient features of history, culture, and geography. This nonideal layer, rooted in the philosophy of nature and history, mobilizes the hypothetical use of reason guided by the regulative, teleological principle of purposiveness [Zweckm\"{a}ssigkeit], rather than the deductive universal claims characteristic of Kant's ideal moral philosophy. RELEVANCE: Directly connects this domain's methodological question (regulative vs. constitutive status of historical teleology) to the broader structure of Kant's political theory, arguing that the "Idea for a Universal History," "Conjectural Beginning," and "Perpetual Peace" together constitute a distinct, regulative-teleological "nonideal" layer of Kantian politics. POSITION: Regulative reading that reframes the entire philosophy-of-history corpus as a coherent "nonideal theory" operating via hypothetical/regulative rather than constitutive reason.},
